module bindings.kotlin.forwardedRoomKeyImportSenderTrust;

// An `m.forwarded_room_key` to-device payload is entirely attacker-shaped: the
// Matrix end-to-end-encryption specification defines it as a key another device
// volunteers, carrying its own `sender_key` and `sender_claimed_ed25519_key`
// fields, and states that a client only acts on one when it answers a
// `m.room_key_request` the client itself sent and comes from a device the client
// already trusts. The claimed fields are not proof of anything on their own --
// only the Curve25519 identity key the Olm envelope was actually encrypted under
// identifies the sender.
//
// In the matrix-android-sdk2 crypto module, MXOlmDevice.addInboundGroupSession is
// the step that commits such a key: it stores the megolm session under the
// claimed `senderKey` and `keysClaimed`, after which every event that session
// decrypts is attributed to that identity. OutgoingKeyRequestManager's
// onRoomKeyForwarded marks the surrounding handler as the forwarded-key path
// rather than the `m.room_key` path a session's own creator sends.
//
// The two store lookups that turn the payload's claims into an authenticated
// decision are IMXCryptoStore.getOutgoingRoomKeyRequest -- was this key ever
// requested -- and IMXCryptoStore.deviceWithIdentityKey, which resolves the
// envelope's identity key to a known device whose verification state can then be
// consulted. A handler that imports a forwarded megolm session without either one
// accepts session key material, and the sender identity bound to it, from
// whichever device the homeserver chooses to deliver (CWE-287, CWE-322).
binding kotlinForwardedMegolmKeyImportWithoutSenderAuthentication {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "call" and node.path ~= "addInboundGroupSession" and containsAny(node.context.scopeCall, ["onRoomKeyForwarded"]) and not (containsAny(node.context.scopeCall, ["getOutgoingRoomKeyRequest", "deviceWithIdentityKey"]))
  emit issue code.ProtocolStateReview at node
}
